    <div class="card-header-split">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">1. Understanding Assessed Value vs. Fair Market Value</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p class="text-body">
        County assessors use mass-appraisal computer models to evaluate tens of thousands of properties simultaneously. These algorithms look at statistical neighborhood trends, square footage, and recorded sales, but they never step inside your front door.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        As a result, computer algorithms frequently miss localized defects, un-updated interior finishes, structural issues, or site dis-amenities (such as heavy traffic noise or slope stability issues). In Washington State, property taxes are levied based on 100 percent of fair market value as of <strong>January 1st</strong> of the assessment year. If your county assessment exceeds what your home would have sold for on the open market on January 1st, you have grounds for an appeal.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="surface-bento">
    <div class="card-header-split">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">2. The Step-by-Step Appeal Process</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="process-step-stack">
      
      <div class="process-step-item">
        <h3 class="process-step-heading">Step A: Review Your Official Value Change Notice</h3>
        <p class="text-body-sm">
          King and Snohomish Counties mail Official Property Value Change Notices between May and September. Note the postmark date immediately. You have until July 1st or 60 days from the notice date (whichever is later) to submit your formal petition.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="process-step-item">
        <h3 class="process-step-heading">Step B: Verify Your County Property Record Card</h3>
        <p class="text-body-sm">
          Look up your parcel number on the King County Assessor or Snohomish County Assessor web portal. Check for factual errors: incorrect square footage, incorrect bedroom or bathroom count, listed acreage errors, or wrong building grade classifications. Correcting factual errors is often the fastest route to a value reduction.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="process-step-item">
        <h3 class="process-step-heading">Step C: Gather 3 to 5 Comparable Sales (Comps)</h3>
        <p class="text-body-sm">
          Identify homes in your immediate sub-neighborhood that sold prior to January 1st of the assessment year. Look for properties with similar age, square footage, style, and lot size that sold for less than your assessed value. (Note: Bank foreclosures, estate sales, and forced family sales are excluded by county boards as non-arm's-length transactions).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="process-step-item">
        <h3 class="process-step-heading">Step D: Document Property Conditions &amp; Defects</h3>
        <p class="text-body-sm">
          If your home requires significant capital repairs (such as a failing roof, cracked foundation, original electrical panel, or severe drainage issues), take clear photos and obtain written repair estimates from licensed contractors. The Board of Equalization will factor documented repair costs directly into valuation adjustments.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="process-step-item">
        <h3 class="process-step-heading">Step E: File Your Official Board of Equalization Petition</h3>
        <p class="text-body-sm">
          Submit your petition online or by mail to the appropriate county board. You do not need to submit all evidence on day one, but your initial petition form must be filed prior to the 60-day deadline. Supplemental evidence can generally be submitted up to 21 business days before your scheduled hearing date.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

